 given statement:\n\n*The **speed** $S$ of an object **decreases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **square of the travel time** $t.$*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *speed* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of the travel time.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the speed *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a247a205-ca5c-5079-820c-cfa34b6f53db" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Constant Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65086243-d80b-56bc-b9cd-f513dd77bfe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281775} {:answer/id #uuid "1f89319f-0dbe-5c11-83ee-be1501623d3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f89319f-0dbe-5c11-83ee-be1501623d3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281776} :answer-field/id #uuid "d203a9ce-bee7-5078-a0ba-971e97ffbe24" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "479b6a87-80fd-5908-88a5-89e26eabce01" :question/math-academy-id "q-347647" :question/problem "The population of maple trees in a park increases by $75$ trees every year. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the population $P(t)$ of the maples? Assume that the time $t$ is measured in years.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If the maple population is $P,$ then the rate of change of the population is $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in years.\n\nSince $75$ new maple trees are added every year, the rate of change of the population of the maples is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=75.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281777 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a247a205-ca5c-5079-820c-cfa34b6f53db" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Constant Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa989acd-9827-50f5-abe0-a1c87b056cb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa989acd-9827-50f5-abe0-a1c87b056cb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281779} :answer-field/id #uuid "67e81cb6-0174-51cd-af4e-179781d14adb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "01d0d014-2d16-55f2-87e9-0d2babf92850" :question/math-academy-id "q-353449" :question/problem "The number of completed online course enrollments increases by $18$ enrollments every hour during a promotional event. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the number of enrollments $E(t)?$ Assume that the time $t$ is measured in hours.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t}=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If the number of enrollments is $E,$ then the rate of change of the number of enrollments is $\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in hours.\n\nSince $18$ new enrollments are added every hour, the rate of change of the number of enrollments is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}E}{\\text{d}t}=18.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281780 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a247a205-ca5c-5079-820c-cfa34b6f53db" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Constant Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "046ded16-6b0e-5ba2-8a3f-0efe2e8b597f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=30T" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281782} {:answer/id #uuid "2f34299d-a0f5-5838-b9b6-bfa4a2ebe106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281783} {:answer/id #uuid "81d4ef73-8e1c-5831-93ef-9a6691697d0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=30t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281784} {:answer/id #uuid "9c3e0078-ed17-5ac9-8deb-824b210a6d3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-30T" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281785} {:answer/id #uuid "4c6f7aa9-1869-5da6-ad24-2359d4c3481c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{30t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f34299d-a0f5-5838-b9b6-bfa4a2ebe106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281783} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ba9eb95-5954-5c8a-9c9b-994c48b6eaf6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3b344ee7-8693-5a88-85d4-4dd538e704ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-38060" :question/problem "An old wall clock loses $30$ seconds every week. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the difference $T$ (in seconds) between the actual time and the time shown on the clock?  Assume that the time $t$ is measured in weeks." :question/worked-solution "If the difference between the actual time and the time shown on the clock is $T$ seconds, then the rate of the change of this difference is $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in weeks.\n\nSince the difference *increases* by $30$ seconds each week, the rate of change of the difference is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=30.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "344f1838-a027-5fd5-82da-17e9b4fcac2b" :knowledge-point/title "Further Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "874fff2e-ad05-545d-b9da-54afafb38522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dD}{dt} = kD, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127257} {:answer/id #uuid "8fbdf968-a3aa-5c7c-bee0-f3e5cdef2d93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dD}{dt} =-kt, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127258} {:answer/id #uuid "0f4bda65-3235-5e5f-bca3-363e844b6c69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dD}{dt} = kt, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127259} {:answer/id #uuid "1d0e73de-fef3-542f-948e-ab7d50987655" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dD}{dt} =-\\frac{k}{t^{2}}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127260} {:answer/id #uuid "77b6d1c9-13bd-55b9-bf57-aaf485a87dc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dD}{dt} = kt^{2}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77b6d1c9-13bd-55b9-bf57-aaf485a87dc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dD}{dt} = kt^{2}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127261} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ce1a994-8031-5aae-8482-b5c5180c0212" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f8683314-e1cb-5e62-aca4-60ca1e1c5bf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-38313" :question/problem "A skater is skating straight down a ramp. The displacement $D$ of the skater increases at a rate that's proportional to the square of the time $t,$ in seconds.\nWhat differential equation describes the rate of change of displacement $D$ at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time since he started skating?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*A skater is skating straight down a ramp. The **displacement** $D$ of the skater **increases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t,$ in seconds. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *displacement* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *square of the time.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}∝{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the displacement *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=k{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "255dee12-5f44-536b-b679-5a894843395d" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00d87778-9f6d-572f-ba24-efb2b01987c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k+V,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281789} {:answer/id #uuid "12cc5a2f-4920-5e47-b94d-215b9f53fb7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kV,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281790} {:answer/id #uuid "7879ec9f-de30-5298-b5ad-eb700ebabf2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=tV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281791} {:answer/id #uuid "32a89d93-5177-5162-bd49-ea2e6085aa05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k-V,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281792} {:answer/id #uuid "999d8f50-9a49-5221-87cf-61d7ddd7de1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=V" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12cc5a2f-4920-5e47-b94d-215b9f53fb7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kV,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281790} :answer-field/id #uuid "d75ca6da-7fe4-5e39-8c9d-57a413cefe1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d093bd1-c5c7-53da-bfe8-604b0e2c79b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-38589" :question/problem "Some people in a community contracted an unknown virus. The number of people infected by the virus increases at a rate that is proportional to the number of people infected by the virus. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the number of people who contracted the virus, $V,$ at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **number of people** infected by the virus **increases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to the **number of people** infected by the virus.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *number of people* infected by the virus varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *number of people* infected. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}∝V.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the number of people infected by the virus  *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kV.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281794 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "255dee12-5f44-536b-b679-5a894843395d" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25f500c2-13a0-5c3f-87db-288c7155abea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=kC,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281796} {:answer/id #uuid "e2fc3eb2-1990-5713-a4cf-b8c49538ffd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=k-C,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281797} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3878d5-5ebd-5d99-bdf6-d73532122c80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281798} {:answer/id #uuid "01962f7b-8aeb-530c-8eec-a68c98758afd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-kC,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281799} {:answer/id #uuid "8159d214-91a2-5e15-8edc-c3af37bb63d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-tC" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01962f7b-8aeb-530c-8eec-a68c98758afd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-kC,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281799} :answer-field/id #uuid "543853c3-44cd-5704-be17-3bb064599976" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69c85e0b-7e0b-5837-bb9c-2bbb4965d5f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-38591" :question/problem "When heated, the concentration $C(t)$ of oxygen in water decreases at a rate that's proportional to the concentration of oxygen in the water at that moment. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the oxygen concentration at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*When heated, the **concentration** $C(t)$ of oxygen in water **decreases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to the **concentration** of oxygen in the water at that moment.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *concentration* of oxygen in water varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *concentration* of oxygen in the water. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}∝C.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the concentration of oxygen *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-kC.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281801 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "255dee12-5f44-536b-b679-5a894843395d" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e0c1b0d-37ec-590a-829a-088678af1976" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kV,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281803} {:answer/id #uuid "29aa10ba-c3bd-58e9-bc47-189de95bbb60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k+V,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281804} {:answer/id #uuid "6ff12420-2ce4-5ca7-9efa-04aaaa67e259" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-tV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281805} {:answer/id #uuid "4f2f9b67-c1e4-5258-a4ff-fc1ad8ecf762" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-V" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281806} {:answer/id #uuid "6408154a-09cb-5601-984b-c6906f78c18e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6408154a-09cb-5601-984b-c6906f78c18e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281807} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1d05de2-5cc1-56a0-8a5d-2c445d638616" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7d02b61-80d9-5e06-9924-32034d7e7b9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-38593" :question/problem "The number of weekly sales of a particular book decreases with time. The number of units sold at any given moment decreases at a rate that's proportional to the number of units sold at that moment. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the number of units sold $V(t)$ at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time since the book first went on sale?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **number of units sold** at any given moment **decreases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to **the number of units sold** at that moment.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *number of units sold* of a particular book varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *number of units sold.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}∝V.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the number of units sold *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129749 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "344f1838-a027-5fd5-82da-17e9b4fcac2b" :knowledge-point/title "Further Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2833a79-c64c-58e2-841e-294e5d5933dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt} =-k\\sqrt{V}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128289} {:answer/id #uuid "ee45cbbe-91ad-5e3a-b2b0-082969f6a84e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt} = \\frac{k}{V^{2}}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128290} {:answer/id #uuid "416f07cf-1706-50fa-8b17-6daf757e4753" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt} =-kV^{2}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128291} {:answer/id #uuid "545a0df9-1298-5759-a415-bd983883c823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt} =-kV, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128292} {:answer/id #uuid "70350c1e-f017-56cc-afb3-45ef43d59f4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt} = kV^{2}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "416f07cf-1706-50fa-8b17-6daf757e4753" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dV}{dt} =-kV^{2}, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128291} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1d3821f-04cc-5ace-979c-d8f8b97466ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2a8b5ef-a26c-5387-8984-3108c19f3b8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-51247" :question/problem "The value $V$ of a particular car decreases with time. The rate of change of the car's value is proportional to the square of the value. What differential equation describes the change of the value at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **value** $V$ of a particular car **decreases** with time. The **rate** of change of the car's value is **proportional** to the **value squared**.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *value* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *value squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}∝{V}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the value *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-k{V}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129750 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a247a205-ca5c-5079-820c-cfa34b6f53db" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Constant Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1e6375c-6122-5dfb-a802-922e2f433f89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt} = 100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128295} {:answer/id #uuid "b351ac2e-260a-5509-944c-936c541c5041" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt} = 100k, k > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128296} {:answer/id #uuid "ba6d0fcd-3941-5f82-8c30-398358e59116" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt} = e^{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128297} {:answer/id #uuid "ff89cf08-e9b9-5480-9c81-69965f9889eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt} = 100 + P" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128298} {:answer/id #uuid "4fed2ed0-c125-5966-a31b-c6784921b876" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt} = 100P" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1e6375c-6122-5dfb-a802-922e2f433f89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt} = 100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128295} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed35efd3-d2cc-5835-a2a3-c99425f3caf4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1cbb7150-61e1-50bf-8ac9-84dfe58c7fcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-51251" :question/problem "The population of pines in a forest increases by $100$ trees every year. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the population $P(t)$ of the pines? Assume that the time $t$ is measured in years." :question/worked-solution "If the pines population is $P,$ then the rate of change of the population is $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in years.\n\nSince $100$ new pines are added every year, the rate of change of the population of the pines is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=100.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "255dee12-5f44-536b-b679-5a894843395d" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51f8e7f5-b543-5151-b6a5-e1edb10f17f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{B},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281810} {:answer/id #uuid "a7f47d35-0b83-5c74-b3b4-3ba60752e64f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=-kB,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281811} {:answer/id #uuid "42fe3c71-23a7-53ff-bd6e-622ba986ab34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{B},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281812} {:answer/id #uuid "b75c7173-c53f-5ebb-973b-4050d1f0febb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281813} {:answer/id #uuid "46aadb23-32a7-52ca-8802-5e4bac17e3db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k-\\frac{1}{B},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7f47d35-0b83-5c74-b3b4-3ba60752e64f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=-kB,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281811} :answer-field/id #uuid "99d16e8d-cf11-5676-87a1-e8b7d6cc7fb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2cfb46d3-f6f4-5c4b-810e-a81336544d7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-51254" :question/problem "The number of bacteria in a particular crop at any given moment decreases at a rate that is proportional to the number of bacteria at that moment.\nWhat differential equation describes the rate of change of the number of bacteria $B$ at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **number of bacteria** in a particular crop at any given moment **decreases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to **the number of bacteria** at that moment..* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *number of bacteria* in a particular crop varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *number of bacteria* at the moment. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}∝B.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the number of bacteria *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=-kB.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a247a205-ca5c-5079-820c-cfa34b6f53db" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Constant Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f89cca23-0e2f-5924-bc2d-999e724ebd3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=200t,\\,\\,t>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281817} {:answer/id #uuid "1890e122-73cc-5d1c-bbeb-b240c860866a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=200D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281818} {:answer/id #uuid "4383e862-7286-5a9f-ac5e-f2d2ef324be7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=-200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281819} {:answer/id #uuid "37c64e21-bff0-5ef4-b0d2-a7b1a97f80ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281820} {:answer/id #uuid "68031ac3-04f3-5e00-865a-97615e114597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{200}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4383e862-7286-5a9f-ac5e-f2d2ef324be7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=-200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281819} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5a60e72-6a15-5995-94e7-ec43acbd730f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a178004-fa27-5003-93fd-a25c987fab4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-79296" :question/problem "A cyclist is getting $200$ meters closer to her destination every minute.\nWhat differential equation describes the rate of change of the distance $D$ (in meters) of the cyclist to her destination? Assume that the time $t$ is measured in minutes." :question/worked-solution "If the remaining distance to the destination is $D$ meters, then the rate of the change of distance is $\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in minutes.\n\nSince the cyclist is $200$ meters closer each minute, the rate of change of remaining distance is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=-200.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is negative because the remaining distance is *decreasing*."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281822 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "255dee12-5f44-536b-b679-5a894843395d" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "794023cb-fbe8-5468-84e7-7e80622db446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}t}=kR,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281824} {:answer/id #uuid "f8716c4b-366e-5c1e-9568-412b62246c78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}t}=-kt,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281825} {:answer/id #uuid "b39e252a-a592-5786-a6c2-195faeb3dc51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}t}=kt,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281826} {:answer/id #uuid "bca2f925-fdf4-5fa2-8f63-1bfd90008886" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}t}=k{t}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281827} {:answer/id #uuid "13e19b9b-df78-5dd2-b4ed-56a0796c6d3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}t}=-kR,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8716c4b-366e-5c1e-9568-412b62246c78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}t}=-kt,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281825} :answer-field/id #uuid "915872be-b99f-54d4-8b43-e85e3bbc3ff0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "abcaf405-19af-5e04-91a4-4a100842c5a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-79316" :question/problem "A cyclist's remaining distance $R$ to their destination decreases at a rate that's proportional to the time, in minutes, since their journey started. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the cyclist's remaining distance at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*A cyclist's **remaining distance** $R$ to a destination **decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to the **time**, in minutes, since their journey started.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *remaining distance* to a destination varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *time* since their journey started. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}t}∝t.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the height *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}t}=-kt.\n$$"}]}
